[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Exception Handling in PL/SQL (Oracle DB)"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Exception handling is a crucial aspect of writing robust and reliable PL/SQL code in Oracle databases. It allows you to gracefully handle unexpected errors or exceptional conditions that may occur during the execution of your program, preventing it from crashing and providing a mechanism for recovery or controlled termination."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"What are Exceptions?"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Exceptions are runtime errors or unexpected events that disrupt the normal flow of a PL/SQL program. They can be caused by various factors, such as invalid data, resource limitations, or programming errors. PL/SQL provides a mechanism to handle these exceptions, allowing you to take appropriate actions when they occur."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"Types of Exceptions"},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"<b>Predefined Exceptions:</b> These are exceptions that are automatically raised by the Oracle database server when specific error conditions occur. Examples include NO_DATA_FOUND (when a SELECT statement returns no rows), TOO_MANY_ROWS (when a SELECT statement returns more than one row into a single variable), and DUP_VAL_ON_INDEX (when attempting to insert a duplicate value into a unique index)."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"<b>User-Defined Exceptions:</b> These are exceptions that you define yourself to handle specific error conditions in your application logic. You can raise these exceptions explicitly using the RAISE statement."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"The Exception Handling Block"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"The basic structure of an exception handling block in PL/SQL is as follows:"},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"DECLARE
  -- Declaration section (variables, cursors, etc.)
BEGIN
  -- Executable statements

EXCEPTION
  WHEN exception_name1 THEN
    -- Handle exception_name1
  WHEN exception_name2 THEN
    -- Handle exception_name2
  WHEN OTHERS THEN
    -- Handle any other exception
END;"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `EXCEPTION` block is optional, but highly recommended. It allows you to specify how to handle different types of exceptions. The `WHEN` clauses specify the exception names to handle, and the corresponding code block is executed when that exception is raised. The `OTHERS` clause is a catch-all handler that handles any exception not explicitly handled by the other `WHEN` clauses. It's good practice to include an `OTHERS` clause to prevent unhandled exceptions from terminating your program abruptly."},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Example: Handling Predefined Exceptions"},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"DECLARE
  v_employee_name VARCHAR2(100);
BEGIN
  SELECT employee_name
  INTO v_employee_name
  FROM employees
  WHERE employee_id = 9999; -- Assuming this ID doesn't exist

  DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('Employee Name: ' || v_employee_name);

EXCEPTION
  WHEN NO_DATA_FOUND THEN
    DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('No employee found with that ID.');
  WHEN TOO_MANY_ROWS THEN
    DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('More than one employee found with that ID.');
  WHEN OTHERS THEN
    DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('An unexpected error occurred.');
END;"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, if the `SELECT` statement returns no rows, the `NO_DATA_FOUND` exception is raised, and the corresponding handler is executed. If the `SELECT` statement returns more than one row (which is not expected in this case), the `TOO_MANY_ROWS` exception is raised. The `OTHERS` clause handles any other unexpected exceptions."},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Example: Handling User-Defined Exceptions"},{"order":16,"type":"code","text":"DECLARE
  invalid_salary EXCEPTION;
  PRAGMA EXCEPTION_INIT(invalid_salary, -20001); -- Associate with an Oracle error code
  v_salary NUMBER := -100;
BEGIN
  IF v_salary < 0 THEN
    RAISE invalid_salary;
  END IF;

  DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('Salary is valid.');

EXCEPTION
  WHEN invalid_salary THEN
    DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('Error: Salary cannot be negative.');
  WHEN OTHERS THEN
    DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('An unexpected error occurred.');
END;"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, we define a user-defined exception called `invalid_salary`. We associate it with a specific Oracle error code using the `PRAGMA EXCEPTION_INIT` directive. This allows us to raise the exception explicitly using the `RAISE` statement when the salary is negative. The `EXCEPTION` block then handles the `invalid_salary` exception."},{"order":18,"type":"subheading","text":"RAISE_APPLICATION_ERROR"},{"order":19,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `RAISE_APPLICATION_ERROR` procedure allows you to raise user-defined error messages with a specific error code. This is a more flexible way to handle errors than simply raising a predefined or user-defined exception."},{"order":20,"type":"code","text":"DECLARE
  v_salary NUMBER := -100;
BEGIN
  IF v_salary < 0 THEN
    RAISE_APPLICATION_ERROR(-20001, 'Salary cannot be negative.');
  END IF;

  DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('Salary is valid.');

EXCEPTION
  WHEN OTHERS THEN
    DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('Error: ' || SQLERRM);
END;"},{"order":21,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, if the salary is negative, we raise an application error with the error code -20001 and the message 'Salary cannot be negative.'. The `SQLERRM` function returns the error message associated with the current exception."},{"order":22,"type":"subheading","text":"Exception Propagation"},{"order":23,"type":"paragraph","text":"If an exception is raised in a PL/SQL block and is not handled within that block, it propagates to the enclosing block. This process continues until the exception is handled or reaches the top-level PL/SQL environment (e.g., a stored procedure called from SQL*Plus), in which case the program terminates with an error."},{"order":24,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices"},{"order":25,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always include an `OTHERS` clause in your exception handling blocks to catch unexpected exceptions."},{"order":26,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Handle exceptions as close to the source of the error as possible."},{"order":27,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use `RAISE_APPLICATION_ERROR` to provide informative error messages to the user."},{"order":28,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Avoid using exceptions for normal program flow control. Exceptions should be reserved for exceptional situations."},{"order":29,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Log exceptions to a table or file for debugging and auditing purposes."},{"order":30,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":31,"type":"paragraph","text":"Exception handling is an essential part of writing robust and maintainable PL/SQL code. By understanding the different types of exceptions and how to handle them, you can create programs that are more resilient to errors and provide a better user experience. Remember to follow best practices to ensure that your exception handling is effective and efficient."}]
